the chief prosecutor of the  international criminal court is  fast-tracking an investigation into  possible war crimes in ukraine after  receiving requests from 39 countries  including canada an advanced team has  left the hague for the ukraine region  moving so fast is unprecedented for the  court to help us understand the  implications i'm joined by payam akkavan  a professor of international law at the  university of toronto and a former u.n  prosecutor professor why do you think  the head prosecutor is moving so fast  well the  invasion of ukraine  is a humanitarian disaster of  unprecedented dimensions and  while the mandate of the prosecutor of  the international criminal court is to  investigate and prosecute international  crimes  one aspect of that mandate is deterrence  basically to try and deter ongoing  violations  so while there appears to be  evidence that war crimes and possibly  crimes against humanity  are being committed  it's important also to signal to the  perpetrators that  the world is watching that their conduct  is going to be scrutinized and that  there may be a price to pay and  hopefully that will uh at least  constrain some of the worst excesses  that may be anticipated in the coming  days  there have been many reports already of  attacks on civilians in ukraine is an  intentional attack on civilians a war  crime  most certainly that is uh the uh most  emphatic war crime  but also indiscriminate attacks are  equally war crimes if you're using  weapons or methods of warfare that fail  to distinguish  between military targets and civilian  population that is also  a war crime and of course  the burden of proof is  more exacting  for establishing intentional attacks  against civilian populations but it's  enough to show that the attacks were  indiscriminate  about the burden of proof what is the  threshold for laying charges of a war  crime or a crime against humanity what  evidence will the investigators be  looking for will they be interviewing  witnesses  well yes the  witness testimony is always central to  establishing such a case  physical evidence video footage  there are multiple sources that go to  establishing a case  beyond a reasonable doubt  in the case of crimes against humanity  of course we're not dealing with  isolated or random war crimes but a  widespread or systematic attack against  the civilian population  so there has to be a significant volume  of evidence which shows that  these are in fact  massive crimes that are being committed  not just at a specific location at a  specific time  but really spread across time and a  wider geographic space  the icc is the only permanent  international court designed to address  genocide wars of aggression war crimes  and crimes against against humanity but  neither ukraine or russia are members of  the court does that matter  well unlike  let's say a canadian court international  courts only have jurisdiction where a  state has consented to that jurisdiction  the ukraine made a declaration after the  russian invasion of crimea in 2014  and it recognized the jurisdiction of  the court russia has not the basis for  jurisdiction  is that the crimes are being committed  on the territory of the ukraine so the  court has jurisdiction even if russia  has no obligation to cooperate  nonetheless the uh prosecutor uh  prosecutor's office will do what it has  to and investigate the crimes and  uh whether states are willing to  cooperate or not is an entirely  different question quick last question  since russia is not a member of the  court and we should point out neither is  the united states it has no legal duty  then to turn over its people for  prosecution so could vladimir putin if  enough evidence is found to convict him  ever face any punishment  well it's a matter of speculation uh who  would be eventually the target of an  investigation  a prosecutor will typically go where the  evidence leads  but once arrest warrants are issued  the estate that is not a party to the  statute of the court may have no  obligation to cooperate but then that  person cannot step foot outside of that  country  in particular in european countries that  are all members  of the international criminal court  because there they will face arrest and  my experience having worked as a u.n  prosecutor in respect of the former  yugoslavia is that people who are in  power today may not be in power tomorrow  and they could eventually face justice  when uh serbian president slobodan  milosevic showed up in the hague in 2001  it was a scene that would have been  unbelievable when the court was  established some years earlier so  in respect of international justice  we have to have a long-term perspective  we have to investigate uh and and issue  arrest warrants  and uh hope that the day will come  when those individuals will face justice  all right professor akavan thank you  thank you
 

